  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Introduction to Logarithms"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What are logarithms?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- A **logarithm** is the **inverse of an exponent**\n\n  - If  $a^{x}=b$  then  `log subscript a open parentheses b close parentheses equals x`,  where  $a>0,b>0,a\\neq1$\n  \n    - This is the essential **definition** of a logarithm\n    - The number $a$ is called the **base** of the logarithm"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Try to get used to **‘reading’ logarithm statements** to yourself\n\n  - $\\mathrm{log}_{a}(b)=x$  means “$x$ is the power that you raise $a$ to, to get $b$\"\n  - So  $\\mathrm{log}_{5}125=3$  means “3 is the power that you raise 5 to, to get 125”"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Two important **special cases** are:\n\n  - `ln space x equals log subscript straight e open parentheses x close parentheses`\n  \n    - Where $e$ is the mathematical constant 2.718…\n    - This is called the **natural logarithm** and will have its own button on your calculator\n  - `log space x equals log subscript 10 open parentheses x close parentheses`\n  \n    - Logarithms of **base 10** are frequently used\n    - They are often abbreviated simply as $\\mathrm{log}x$"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "Why use logarithms?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Logarithms allow us to **solve equations** where the **exponent** is the unknown value\n\n  - We can solve some of these by **inspection**\n  \n    - For example, for the equation  $2^{x}=8$  we know that $x$* *must be $3$\n  - But logarithms allow us to solve **more complicated** problems\n  \n    - For example, the equation  $2^{x}=10$  does not have an obvious answer\n    - Instead we can **rewrite** the equation as a logarithm"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$\\mathrm{log}_{2}10=x$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- and use our **calculator** to find the decimal value of $\\mathrm{log}_{2}10$"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$x=3.321928...$"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "exam-tip",
   "label": "Exam Hint",
   "md": "- Make sure you are completely familiar with your calculator's logarithm functions"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "Solve the following equations:\n\ni) $x=\\mathrm{log}_{3}27$,\n*Use the definition of a logarithm to rewrite this as an exponential equation*\n\n> *Remember, this equation means \"*$x$* is the power that you raise 3 to, to get 27\"*\n\n$3^{x}=27$\n\n> *This can be solved by inspection*\n\n$3^{3}=27$\n\n$x=3$\n\nii) $2^{x}=21.4$, giving your answer to 3 s.f.\n\n> *Use the definition of a logarithm to rewrite this as a logarithm equation*\n\n> *We want the logarithm that means \"*$x$* is the power that you raise 2 to, to get 21.4\"*\n\n$x=\\mathrm{log}_{2}21.4$\n\n> *Use your calculator to find the value of that logarithm*\n\n$x=4.419538...$\n\n> *Round to 3 significant figures*\n\n> $x=4.42$* **(3 s.f.)*"
  },
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_r82CHwYg4ZxfDTgc",
   "name": "Logarithmic Functions & Graphs",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Logarithmic Functions & Graphs"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What is a logarithmic function?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- A l**ogarithmic function** is of the form $f(x)=\\mathrm{log}_{a}x,x>0$\n\n  - In this course the **base** $a$ for a logarithmic function will always be an **integer greater than one**\n- Its **domain** is the set of all **positive real numbers**\n\n  - You can't take a log of zero or a negative number\n- Its **range** is the set of **all real numbers**\n- $\\mathrm{log}_{a}x$ and $a^{x}$ are **inverse** functions\n\n  - `log subscript a open parentheses a to the power of x close parentheses equals x`  and  $a^{\\mathrm{log}_{a}x}=x$"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What are the key features of logarithmic graphs?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The graph of  $y=\\mathrm{log}_{a}x$\n\n  - **does not have a **$y$**-intercept**\n  - has a **vertical asymptote **at the *y*-axis: $x=0$\n  - has **one **$x$**-intercept** at (1, 0)\n  - **passes through** the point (*a*, 1)\n  - **does not have any minimum or maximum points**"
  },
  {
   "type": "figure",
   "orig_src": "https://cdn.savemyexams.com/uploads/2024/03/FsosVIe~_logarithm-graph.png",
